      "problem": "One month, a greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon. If the number of kilograms of sold watermelon doubles each month, how many kilograms of watermelon will the greengrocer sell after $4$ months?",
      "worked_solution": "In a particular month, the greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon, and then this amount doubles every month.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ month, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot 2$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $2$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{2}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $3$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{3}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $4$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{4}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\nSo the correct expression is $40\\cdot {2}^{4}.$",
